course: dictionary
lesson: 822
title: Bell in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. «A girl in Vermont plays on the icy playground: when the bell rings she stops at once and lines up.» Here a bell is a signal, a sound that tells children to stop and line up. It is not a metal cup.
explain: «Cats are hunters by instinct; a bell on the collar can warn birds and mice.» Here a bell is a small metal cup that rings when it is shaken. The clue is the collar: you can wear a metal cup on a collar, but a signal cannot be worn.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. Try "the signal rings" or "the cup rings" and see which one makes sense. Clues such as rings, collar and lines up show you which meaning the writer chose.
explain: Bell also appears in phrases, such as ring a bell, which means seem familiar, and saved by the bell. A phrase is a group of words with its own meaning, which may not match the single words. Look at the whole group before you decide.
fact: A bell is a hollow metal thing, often shaped like a cup, that rings when you hit it or shake it.
fact: A bell can also be anything that makes a ringing sound to give a signal, like a doorbell, a bike bell or the school bell.
fact: The ringing itself can be called a bell too, as in wait for the bell.
fact: In maths, you might count how many times a bell rings.
fact: In science, a bell vibrates to make sound.
exercise: «When the bell rings she stops at once and lines up.» In this sentence, what is a bell? | A cat's collar | A letter sent by messenger | *A signal that tells children to stop and line up | A hollow metal cup for holding tea
exercise: «A bell on the collar can warn birds and mice.» What is the bell here? | *A small metal cup that rings when shaken | A school signal at the end of the day | A telephone that sends messages | A coded click sent along a wire
exercise: «Again?!» says Mrs Bell. Is Bell a name or a thing here? | A sound from a doorbell | A ringing signal | *A name | A metal cup
test: Which phrase means seem familiar? | Wait for the cup | Shake a bell | Hit a drum | *Ring a bell
test: In science, what does a bell do to make sound? | *Vibrates | Freezes | Dissolves | Melts
exam: «Alexander Graham Bell made a working telephone in 1876.» In this sentence, Bell is | A signal | A school bell | A small metal cup | *A name
